### What is the problem?

Given two strings A and B, find the minimum number of deletions from A and insertions into it to transform A into B. Only deletions and insertions are allowed, not substitutions. For A="HEAP" and B="PEA", the LCS is "EA" (length 2), so deletions = 4-2 = 2 (remove H, P), insertions = 3-2 = 1 (add P at the end if needed), though the actual operations depend on the reconstruction.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class MinDeleteInsert {
    public static int[] minOperations(String a, String b) {
        int m = a.length(), n = b.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];
        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    dp[i][j] = 1 + dp[i - 1][j - 1];
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
                }
            }
        }
        int lcs = dp[m][n];
        int deletions = m - lcs;
        int insertions = n - lcs;
        return new int[]{deletions, insertions};
    }
}
```

### Why does this reduce to the LCS?

Any character belonging to the LCS appears in both strings in the same relative order and can stay in place during the transformation. Every character in A that is not part of the LCS must be deleted. Every character in B that is not part of the LCS must be inserted. So both operation counts derive from the single LCS length.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class ReductionDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String a = "ABCDEF", b = "AZBDCF";
        int[] ops = MinDeleteInsert.minOperations(a, b);
        int lcs = a.length() - ops[0];
        System.out.println("A: " + a + " (length " + a.length() + ")");
        System.out.println("B: " + b + " (length " + b.length() + ")");
        System.out.println("LCS length: " + lcs);
        System.out.println("Deletions from A: " + ops[0]);
        System.out.println("Insertions for B: " + ops[1]);
    }
}
```

```text
A: ABCDEF (length 6)
B: AZBDCF (length 6)
LCS length: 4
Deletions from A: 2
Insertions for B: 2
```

### How does the LCS DP work?

The table dp[i][j] holds the LCS length of the first i characters of A and the first j characters of B. On a match, add 1 to the diagonal. On a mismatch, keep the max of the cell above or to the left. The LCS length is in dp[m][n].

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class LCSDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String a = "OLD", b = "NEW";
        int[][] dp = new int[a.length() + 1][b.length() + 1];
        for (int i = 1; i <= a.length(); i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= b.length(); j++) {
                dp[i][j] = a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)
                    ? 1 + dp[i - 1][j - 1]
                    : Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
            }
        }
        int lcs = dp[a.length()][b.length()];
        System.out.println("LCS(" + a + ", " + b + ") = " + lcs);
        System.out.println("Deletions: " + (a.length() - lcs));
        System.out.println("Insertions: " + (b.length() - lcs));
    }
}
```

```text
LCS(OLD, NEW) = 0
Deletions: 3
Insertions: 3
```

### How is this different from edit distance?

Edit distance (Levenshtein distance) allows three operations: insert, delete, and substitute. This problem allows only two: insert and delete. Without substitution, transforming one character into another requires one deletion plus one insertion (cost 2), whereas edit distance counts that as one substitution (cost 1). The insert-delete problem maps directly to the LCS.

| Problem | Operations | Uses |
|---|---|---|
| This problem | Delete + Insert | LCS |
| Edit distance | Delete + Insert + Substitute | DP with 3 choices |
| Total ops here | (m - LCS) + (n - LCS) | m + n - 2*LCS |

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class VsEditDistanceDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String a = "ABC", b = "AXC";
        int[] ops = MinDeleteInsert.minOperations(a, b);
        System.out.println("Delete+Insert: " + ops[0] + " deletions + " + ops[1] + " insertions = " + (ops[0] + ops[1]) + " total");
        // Edit distance with substitution would be 1 (replace B with X)
        System.out.println("Edit distance would be: 1 (substitute B→X)");
    }
}
```

```text
Delete+Insert: 1 deletions + 1 insertions = 2 total
Edit distance would be: 1 (substitute B→X)
```

### What are the edge cases?

If both strings are identical, LCS equals their length, so 0 deletions and 0 insertions. If they share no common character, all of A is deleted and all of B is inserted. If one string is empty, all operations are insertions or deletions.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class EdgeCaseDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] same = MinDeleteInsert.minOperations("ABC", "ABC");
        System.out.println("Same: del=" + same[0] + " ins=" + same[1]);

        int[] diff = MinDeleteInsert.minOperations("ABC", "XYZ");
        System.out.println("All diff: del=" + diff[0] + " ins=" + diff[1]);

        int[] empty = MinDeleteInsert.minOperations("", "ABC");
        System.out.println("Empty→ABC: del=" + empty[0] + " ins=" + empty[1]);
    }
}
```

```text
Same: del=0 ins=0
All diff: del=3 ins=3
Empty→ABC: del=0 ins=3
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

Time is O(m*n) for the LCS computation. Space is O(m*n) for the table, reducible to O(min(m,n)) with rolling rows. Both answers come from a single computation.

### What are common mistakes?

Forgetting that the total operations are deletions plus insertions, not just one of them. Confusing this with edit distance which allows substitutions. Using the common substring instead of the common subsequence, which would give a wrong LCS value.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How would you reconstruct the actual sequence of operations?
- How does this relate to the shortest common supersequence problem?
- What if deletions and insertions have different costs?
- How would you solve this with edit distance allowing substitutions?
